module sh.sysl.ui

import sysl.buf.{Buf, buf}

// What a frame is drawn through, and what tells a view how big its text is.
//
// **This is a trait rather than a struct because the probe now has two backends and they are not
// alike.** The recorder below answers every question about the *tree* with no window, no font and
// no display server, which is what made five of card 0203's six questions answerable in a unit
// test. The sixth is about a real frame, and a real frame needs a real font -- so the thing a view
// draws through has to be something a caller supplies.
//
// **A view reaches it as `*Canvas`, an erased pointer, and that is the shape rather than a generic
// bound.** `imui` takes `p: *P` for `[P: Painter]` so every call monomorphizes and nothing is
// dispatched; it can, because its widgets are functions. A `View` here is already a trait object,
// and a generic method would make it one no longer -- so the canvas is erased too, and the two
// erasures meet at one indirect call per drawing operation. That is the cost of a retained tree and
// it is the bargain 0168 struck.
//
// **Measurement is on this trait and not on the view**, which is the one thing the recorder version
// of the probe got wrong: `Text` measured itself at eight pixels a byte, and a row of a list is
// however tall the font says it is. A canvas knows its font; a view does not and should not.
//
// **EVERY OPERATION HERE IS DRAWABLE WITH FILLED RECTANGLES AND A GLYPH RUN, AND THAT IS THE
// CONSTRAINT RATHER THAN AN ACCIDENT.** The toolkit has to run where there is no vector graphics
// library at all -- a phone, where the backend is plain SDL3 and nothing else -- so a rounded corner
// here is spans of pixels a backend fills row by row, not a path handed to a rasterizer. A backend
// that *does* have one is free to use it, and gets the same picture; a backend that does not is
// never asked for something it cannot draw.
//
// That is what keeps this surface small, and small is what makes a second backend cheap. Anything
// that cannot be expressed as rectangles, a glyph run and a clip belongs in a layer above this one.
//
// **`draw_image` is the one exception, and it is an exception to the letter rather than the spirit.**
// A photograph is not a run of filled rectangles at any price, so a program wanting one had no way
// down to the backend drawing its window -- but putting a block of pixels somewhere is the other
// thing every backend can already do, a plain SDL3 one included, and it needs no path and no
// rasterizer. What is still refused here is a *shape* a backend would have to be able to trace.
trait Canvas
    // Fill a rectangle with a solid colour, 0xRRGGBB, its corners rounded by `radius`.
    //
    // **A fill and a stroke rather than one styled box**, because the modifiers are separate and a
    // backend should not be asked to draw what nobody wrote: `.background(c, 8)` fills and
    // `.border(c, 1, 8)` strokes, and a view wearing both makes two calls in the order the chain put
    // them. There is no colour meaning "none", and this is why one is not needed.
    //
    // **The radius is a defaulted parameter rather than a second member**, which is worth stating
    // because the surface carried both for a day: a square fill is a rounded one with no radius, and
    // two members meant every backend wrote the same body twice and every view chose between them.
    // A default on a trait member is filled in at the call, including a call through `*Canvas` where
    // the receiver has forgotten its type -- so `c.fill(r, colour)` costs a backend nothing.
    fill(*self, r: Rect, color: u32, radius: int = 0)

    // Draw a run of text with its top-left at `r`, clipped to `r`.
    text(*self, r: Rect, t: string)

    // Outline a rectangle, `width` pixels thick, drawn **inside** `r` and rounded by `radius`.
    //
    // Inside rather than centred on the edge, so a bordered view occupies exactly the rectangle
    // layout gave it and never bleeds into a neighbour -- which is what lets a border be added to a
    // view without moving anything around it.
    // **`dash` is one number rather than a pattern**, and the pattern it means is that many pixels
    // on and that many off. A general dash array would be a slice a backend has to keep alive past
    // the call and a shape nothing in the toolkit asks for; what a dashed border is *for* is saying
    // "provisional" at a glance, and an even dash says it.
    stroke(*self, r: Rect, color: u32, width: int = 1, radius: int = 0, dash: int = 0)

    // Restrict drawing to `r` until the matching `unclip`.
    clip(*self, r: Rect)

    unclip(*self)

    // Is any part of `r` still reachable, given whatever clipping is in force?
    //
    // **This is what lets a container skip a child instead of drawing one nobody can see**, and it
    // is the only thing standing between a list and paying for every row it holds. A scrolling list
    // of three hundred rows shows about ten; the other two hundred and ninety are painted, clipped
    // away by the backend, and thrown out after all the work of producing them. Measured at 900x660:
    // that was 2.8 ms a frame, seventeen per cent of a 60 Hz budget, spent entirely on pixels that
    // were discarded.
    //
    // **A container asks before it paints and not before it measures**, because it still has to know
    // how tall each child is to know where the next one starts. What culling removes is the drawing,
    // which is nearly all of the cost.
    //
    // **The default is `true`, so a backend that tracks no clip is correct and merely slower.** That
    // is the right way round: a backend which answered `false` by mistake would draw nothing at all,
    // and this way the failure mode of not implementing it is a frame that is right.
    visible(*self, r: Rect) -> bool = true

    // What a control looks like when nobody has said, and the two that change it for a subtree.
    //
    // **On the canvas for the same reason the pointer and the phases are: the tree has nowhere to
    // keep anything.** A theme has to be reachable at `measure` and at `paint`, from a node that
    // was built before anything knew what it would be drawn into, and the canvas is the only thing
    // present at both moments that outlives the tree.
    //
    // **All three have bodies, so a backend owes nothing.** One that implements none of them draws
    // every frame in `dark()` and a `.restyle(...)` in the tree quietly has no effect -- which is
    // the same bargain `visible` strikes and the same way round: not implementing it costs the
    // feature and never the frame. A backend that wants theming keeps a `Theme` and a `Buf[Theme]`,
    // which is what `Recorder` and `PlutoCanvas` both do in four lines.
    theme(*self) -> Theme = dark()

    push_theme(*self, t: Theme) = ()

    pop_theme(*self) = ()

    // Override the colour text is drawn in, until the matching `unink`.
    //
    // **What text is drawn in when nothing has said otherwise is the THEME's `ink`**, and that is
    // the important half. A backend that carried its own default text colour had one the theme could
    // not reach -- so a dialog raised above a restyled tree came out in the colour the backend was
    // constructed with, which on a light interface is invisible. This is a stack of *overrides*
    // over the theme, not a colour of its own.
    //
    // **Text colour is canvas state rather than a parameter on `text`, and that is what makes a
    // hover effect possible at all.** A tree is built before anything knows where the pointer is,
    // so a node cannot be given the colour it will need; what it can do is carry every appearance
    // it might take and choose while painting. A styled view sets the ink around its child, so the
    // label inside a button changes with the button and nothing had to hand it a colour.
    //
    // It nests like `clip`: a view restores what it found rather than assuming a default.
    ink(*self, color: u32)

    unink(*self)

    // The colour text would be drawn in right now: whatever `ink` pushed over it, or the theme's.
    //
    // **The default body is the whole rule** -- a backend that pushes nothing and remembers nothing
    // still draws text in the theme's ink, which is what makes a tree with no `foreground` anywhere
    // readable under any theme. A backend carrying a default text colour of its own would be
    // carrying one no theme could reach.
    text_ink(*self) -> u32 = self.theme().ink

    // Is the pointer inside `r` right now, and is it held down there?
    //
    // **Asked during paint, which is the only moment a view knows its rectangle.** Layout is
    // decided inside `paint` and gone by the time it returns, so a question about *this* view's
    // geometry has to be asked while the geometry exists -- the same reasoning that put hit regions
    // in the paint pass rather than in a walk of their own.
    //
    // A backend that has no pointer -- a recorder, a printer, an offscreen render -- answers
    // `false` to both, and a tree drawn through one looks the way it looks at rest.
    hovered(*self, r: Rect) -> bool

    pressed(*self, r: Rect) -> bool

    // Does this rectangle own the pointer, having been the one the press began in?
    //
    // **This is what `pressed` cannot do, and the difference is a drag that works.** `pressed` is
    // inside-and-held, so a slider read through it abandons its drag the moment the pointer strays
    // above the track -- and a scrollbar thumb, eight pixels wide, is left almost at once. A browser
    // captures the pointer at the press and keeps sending it to that element until the release, and
    // a person has decades of expecting that.
    //
    // **Ask this and then ask `pointer()`**, which may by then be anywhere at all -- outside the
    // rectangle, outside the window. Clamping what comes back is the caller's, and it is the same
    // clamp a drag inside the track already needed.
    //
    // `slot` tells two grabbable things sharing a rectangle apart, the way it does for `phase`.
    //
    // **The default is `pressed`, so a backend that tracks no press point still works** -- it just
    // asks the pointer to stay inside, which is what every widget did before this existed. Not
    // implementing it costs the convenience and never the frame.
    grab(*self, r: Rect, slot: int) -> bool = self.pressed(r)

    // Where the pointer actually is, in the same coordinates a view is painted in.
    //
    // **`hovered` and `pressed` answer a yes-or-no question and a drag is not one.** A slider has to
    // know *where along itself* the pointer is, and no amount of asking "are you inside this
    // rectangle" produces a position -- so the one widget that reads a continuous value from the
    // pointer needs the pointer, and it is the only thing in the toolkit that does.
    //
    // **The default is off the surface**, which is the same answer `hovered` gives from a backend
    // with no pointer: a printer, an offscreen render, a recorder that was never told. A widget
    // reading it gets a position no rectangle contains, which is what "nothing is pointing at this"
    // has to look like when the answer is a coordinate rather than a flag.
    pointer(*self) -> (int, int) = (-1, -1)

    // How far an animation on this rectangle has travelled toward `target`, moving at `rate` per
    // second. `slot` tells two animations on one view apart.
    //
    // **This is on the canvas for the same reason the pointer is: the tree has nowhere to keep it.**
    // A `Reactive` is rebuilt on every paint, so anything it stored would be thrown away between the
    // frame that started an animation and the frame that continues it. The canvas is the one thing
    // that lives across frames and already knows the rectangle, which is what `phases.sysl` is and
    // what its own prose argues at length.
    //
    // **The default answers `target`, so a backend with no clock is drawn settled** -- a printer, an
    // offscreen render, a test that says nothing about time. That is what makes animation something
    // a backend opts into rather than something every backend owes.
    phase(*self, r: Rect, slot: int, target: real, rate: real) -> real = target

    // Where the press that owns the pointer began, in the same coordinates a view is painted in.
    //
    // **This is what makes selecting text stateless.** A drag has two ends -- where it started and
    // where it is now -- and a view that had to remember the first would need somewhere to keep it
    // between frames, which is the one thing the tree cannot do. The canvas already knows, because
    // capturing the pointer meant recording where the press began.
    press_origin(*self) -> (int, int) = (-1, -1)

    // ## The keyboard, which is the one thing a rectangle cannot key
    //
    // **A click carries a position and a key carries nothing**, so where a key goes is decided by
    // state that outlived the previous frame rather than by geometry. That state is focus, there is
    // one of it, and `key.sysl` argues at length why the key for it is an id the caller picks rather
    // than the rectangle everything else here is keyed by.
    //
    // **All five default to a canvas with no keyboard**, which draws an editable field as an
    // ordinary run of text that cannot be typed into -- a plainer picture rather than a wrong one,
    // which is the rule every other defaulted member on this trait follows.

    // Which view has the keyboard, or `NOBODY`.
    focus(*self) -> int = NOBODY

    // Take it. A view calls this while painting, on the frame a press lands inside it.
    take_focus(*self, id: int) = ()

    // What arrived since the last frame, for whichever view has the focus.
    keys(*self) -> []Press = no_keys()

    // Did the press this frame follows land in `r`, and where exactly?
    //
    // **This is not `pressed(r)`, and a touch screen is why.** A mouse press lasts for several
    // frames, so a view could notice one while painting; a tap is a press and a release between two
    // frames, and the paint in the middle sees nothing at all. The press is therefore recorded when
    // it happens and reported for exactly the one frame that follows it.
    tapped(*self, r: Rect) -> bool = false

    // Where that press was, in the coordinates a view is painted in.
    tap_point(*self) -> (int, int) = (-1, -1)

    // Where the focused view's caret is, and what it has selected.
    editing(*self) -> Selection = nothing_selected()

    // Put it somewhere else, which is what a key or a click in the focused view does.
    edit(*self, sel: Selection) = ()

    // Forget the keys this frame delivered. **`render` calls it, and nothing else should**: a key
    // belongs to exactly one frame, and where a frame ends is the one thing a canvas cannot see.
    keys_done(*self) = ()

    // The byte offset in `s` of the character boundary nearest `x` pixels from the run's left edge.
    //
    // **The default body is written in terms of `text_size`, so no backend has to implement it** --
    // a canvas that can measure a run can measure its prefixes, and that is all a hit test is. It
    // walks characters and builds the prefix as it goes, which is how the offset comes out on a
    // character boundary and never inside a multi-byte one.
    //
    // It is linear in the length of the run and allocates a prefix per character, which is honest
    // rather than good: a backend with a real shaper knows the answer directly and should override
    // this. It is not on a per-frame path -- only a drag asks.
    //
    // **Past the middle of a character snaps past it**, which is what makes a click land where a
    // person points rather than always before the letter they aimed at.
    text_at(*self, s: string, x: int) -> usize
        if x <= 0 then return 0usize

        var prefix = ""
        var before = 0

        for ch in s.chars
            val next = prefix + s"${ch}"
            val after = self.text_size(next).w

            if x < (before + after) / 2 then return prefix.len
            if x < after then return next.len

            prefix = next
            before = after

        s.len
    end text_at

    // How far into the run the boundary at byte offset `at` is.
    //
    // The same default trick: a prefix is a run, and a run can be measured.
    text_x(*self, s: string, at: usize) -> int =
        if at >= s.len then self.text_size(s).w else self.text_size(s[0..<at]).w

    // How much room `s` takes in this canvas's font. A view measures through this or it is guessing.
    //
    // **`*self` rather than `self` because a real canvas has a cache.** Measuring a run of text is a
    // shaping pass, and a backend that answers the same question sixty times a second wants to
    // remember; a by-value receiver would copy the canvas -- its texture cache, its renderer handle
    // and its hit list -- once per measurement to avoid a mutation it is entitled to make.
    text_size(*self, s: string) -> Size

    // Put a picture into `r`, scaled to fill it, with its corners rounded by `radius`.
    //
    // **The default draws nothing**, which is the rule every optional member here follows: a backend
    // with no way to put pixels down produces a frame with the picture missing, which is a plainer
    // frame rather than a wrong one. `image.sysl` argues why a fourth drawing operation was worth
    // adding at all -- a photograph is not expressible as filled rectangles at any price.
    draw_image(*self, r: Rect, img: &Image, radius: int = 0) = ()

    // Paint this view after the whole tree has been painted, above everything, at `at`.
    //
    // **Everything else composes downward and this is the one thing that does not.** A dropdown is
    // anchored to something small and has to spill out over what comes after it; a dialog covers the
    // window from somewhere deep inside it. Raising is how: by the time these are painted every
    // clip has been unwound, so an overlay is unclipped without asking, and because the hit list is
    // last-wins it is on top for input without asking either.
    //
    // **This has no default body, unlike every other member added since the trait was written.** The
    // rule elsewhere is that not implementing something costs the feature and never the frame -- a
    // backend that ignores `visible` is merely slower, one that ignores `grab` merely asks the
    // pointer to stay inside. A backend that ignored this would draw a frame with the dialog
    // *missing*, which is a wrong picture rather than a plainer one, so it is required and a new
    // backend is made to think about it.
    raise_above(*self, v: &View, at: Rect)

    // **The theme and the text colour in force are captured with it**, so an overlay paints as
    // though it were still where it was raised rather than in whatever the root was drawn in. The
    // clip is the one thing deliberately left behind, which is the whole point of raising.
    //
    // Take what has been raised, clearing it. `render` is what calls this.
    take_overlays(*self) -> Buf[Overlay]

    // Say that `r` answers to a tap, and what to do about it.
    //
    // **Input is collected by the paint pass rather than by a pass of its own**, which is the
    // cheapest thing that works over an erased tree: there is no element to hang a hit region on
    // and no second walk that would know where anything landed, because layout is decided during
    // paint. So a view that wants a tap says so where it draws, the canvas collects the regions in
    // paint order, and the backend matches a click against the last one that contains it -- last
    // being topmost, since a later fill covers an earlier one.
    hit(*self, r: Rect, f: &Fn() -> unit)
end Canvas

// A backend that draws nothing and remembers everything.
//
// **This is what lets the probe answer its questions with no window.** A tree that laid itself out
// correctly and a tree that did not look identical from the outside unless something records where
// each piece landed; `imui` reached the same conclusion and has `record.sysl` for it.
//
// The recording is text on purpose. A structured command list would need an enum, an `Eq` and a
// printer before a single assertion could be written, and what a failing test wants to show is a
// line a person can read.
struct Recorder
    ops: Buf[string]

    // What the frame said was interactive, so a test can click a button rather than assert that a
    // line naming one was printed.
    taps: Hits

    // The font this canvas pretends to draw with, one fixed advance and one line height.
    //
    // **It is a parameter rather than a constant so that a test can lay a tree out against a font
    // it was not written for.** That is not idle: a view measured itself until a real frame loop
    // showed a list of rows in the wrong places, and the reason nothing caught it was that the only
    // canvas in existence agreed with the guess. Two fonts is what makes the disagreement visible.
    char_w: int
    line_h: int

    // Where the pointer is and whether it is held down, which is what `hovered` and `pressed`
    // answer from.
    //
    // **It starts off the surface**, so a tree drawn through a fresh recorder looks the way it looks
    // at rest -- a test that says nothing about the pointer gets the resting appearance rather than
    // whatever state the origin happens to fall in.
    px: int
    py: int
    down: bool

    // The theme in force and the ones to go back to, which is `tints`' discipline applied to
    // everything a theme carries.
    look: Theme
    looks: Buf[Theme]

    // The clip rectangles in force, innermost last, so that `visible` can answer and a test can
    // watch a container skip what it cannot draw.
    //
    // Each entry is already intersected with the one below it, so the innermost is the whole answer.
    clips: Buf[Rect]

    // Who owns the pointer while it is held, so a drag can be tested leaving the thing it drags.
    hold: Grab

    // What has been raised above the tree and not yet painted.
    raised: Buf[Overlay]

    // The text colours pushed over the theme's, innermost last. Empty means "whatever the theme
    // says", which is what makes a tree with no `foreground` in it readable under any theme.
    tints: Buf[u32]

    // The animations in flight, so that easing can be tested without a clock or a window.
    //
    // **A test drives time by hand**, which is the only way an animation is testable at all: a
    // fixed sequence of `tick`s is reproducible where a real frame loop is not, and a test asserting
    // what a hover looks like a tenth of a second in should not depend on how busy the machine was.
    anim: Phases

    // Who has the keyboard, what it has been sent, and where its caret is.
    board: Keyboard

    // Put the pointer somewhere, so a test can hover and press without a window.
    point(*self, x: int, y: int, down: bool = false)
        self.px = x
        self.py = y
        self.down = down
        self.hold.moved(x, y, down)
        self.board.moved(x, y, down)
    end point

    // Send a key, so a test can type without a window.
    //
    // **Nothing clears these between paints unless a test asks**, which is the opposite of what a
    // whole frame does and right for a test: a recording is usually one paint long, and a key that
    // vanished before the field was drawn would be a test that silently asserted nothing. A test
    // wanting the frame-loop behaviour paints through `render`, which ends the frame's keys itself.
    key(*self, p: Press) = self.board.press(p)

    // Advance the clock by `dt` seconds. Until this is called nothing moves, so a recording made
    // without it shows the settled appearance.
    tick(*self, dt: real) = self.anim.tick(dt)

    // What was drawn, oldest first, one operation per line.
    dump(self) -> string
        var out = ""
        val v = self.ops.view()

        for i in 0..<v.len
            out = out + v[i] + "\n"

        out
    end dump
end Recorder

// A radius, written only when there is one.
private radii(radius: int) -> string = if radius == 0 then "" else s" r${radius}"

// **A fixed advance a byte, and it is a lie meant to be one.** A real font measures with
// `TTF_GetStringSize`; this is stable, which is what a test wants, and wrong for anything but
// ASCII, which is what keeps anybody from mistaking it for a font.
impl Canvas for Recorder
    // **A square fill records as it always did**, so that the radius does not appear in the reading
    // of a tree that never asked for one -- a test about layout should not have to say anything
    // about corners. A rounded one adds `r8`, and that is the whole of the difference.
    fill(*self, r: Rect, color: u32, radius: int)
        self.ops.push(s"fill ${r.x},${r.y} ${r.w}x${r.h} #${color}" + radii(radius))

    stroke(*self, r: Rect, color: u32, width: int, radius: int, dash: int)
        self.ops.push(s"stroke ${r.x},${r.y} ${r.w}x${r.h} #${color} w${width}" + radii(radius) +
                      (if dash == 0 then "" else s" d${dash}"))

    text(*self, r: Rect, t: string)
        self.ops.push(s"text ${r.x},${r.y} ${r.w}x${r.h} '${t}'")

    clip(*self, r: Rect)
        val v = self.clips.view()

        self.ops.push(s"clip ${r.x},${r.y} ${r.w}x${r.h}")
        self.clips.push(if v.len == 0 then r else meet(v[v.len - 1], r))

    unclip(*self)
        self.ops.push("unclip")
        self.clips.pop()

    override visible(*self, r: Rect) -> bool
        val v = self.clips.view()

        v.len == 0 || r.overlaps(v[v.len - 1])

    override text_ink(*self) -> u32
        val v = self.tints.view()

        if v.len == 0 then self.look.ink else v[v.len - 1]
    end text_ink

    override theme(*self) -> Theme = self.look

    override push_theme(*self, t: Theme)
        self.looks.push(self.look)
        self.look = t

    override pop_theme(*self)
        self.looks.pop() match
            Some(t) -> self.look = t
            None -> ()

    ink(*self, color: u32)
        self.ops.push(s"ink #${color}")
        self.tints.push(color)

    unink(*self)
        self.ops.push("unink")
        self.tints.pop()

    hovered(*self, r: Rect) -> bool = r.holds(self.px, self.py)

    pressed(*self, r: Rect) -> bool = self.down && r.holds(self.px, self.py)

    override pointer(*self) -> (int, int) = (self.px, self.py)

    override grab(*self, r: Rect, slot: int) -> bool = self.hold.claim(r, slot)

    override press_origin(*self) -> (int, int) = (self.hold.began_x, self.hold.began_y)

    override focus(*self) -> int = self.board.focus

    override take_focus(*self, id: int) = self.board.take(id)

    override keys(*self) -> []Press = self.board.keys()

    override tapped(*self, r: Rect) -> bool = self.board.landed(r)

    override tap_point(*self) -> (int, int) = (self.board.tap_x, self.board.tap_y)

    override editing(*self) -> Selection = self.board.sel

    override edit(*self, sel: Selection) = self.board.edit(sel)

    override keys_done(*self) = self.board.reset()

    // The trait's own body answers `target`, which is the settled appearance. A recorder has a store
    // and a clock a test can drive, so it replaces it.
    override phase(*self, r: Rect, slot: int, target: real, rate: real) -> real =
        self.anim.at(r, slot, target, rate)

    text_size(*self, s: string) -> Size = Size(int(s.len) * self.char_w, self.line_h)

    hit(*self, r: Rect, f: &Fn() -> unit)
        self.ops.push(s"hit ${r.x},${r.y} ${r.w}x${r.h}")
        self.taps.add(r, f)

    // **The picture's own size is recorded beside the rectangle it went into**, because that is the
    // half a fit decides and the half a test is about: a recording that said only where it landed
    // could not tell `Contain` from `Stretch`.
    override draw_image(*self, r: Rect, img: &Image, radius: int)
        self.ops.push(s"image ${r.x},${r.y} ${r.w}x${r.h} of ${img.width()}x${img.height()}" +
                      radii(radius))

    // **Recorded as well as kept**, so a test can see that something was raised without having to
    // run a whole frame through `render` -- which is the difference between asserting that a menu
    // opens and asserting what the menu looks like.
    raise_above(*self, v: &View, at: Rect)
        self.ops.push(s"raise ${at.x},${at.y} ${at.w}x${at.h}")
        self.raised.push(Overlay(v, at, self.look, override_ink(self.tints)))

    take_overlays(*self) -> Buf[Overlay]
        val out = self.raised

        self.raised = buf()
        out
    end take_overlays
end Recorder

// The default font is eight by sixteen, which is what every measurement in the package's own tests
// is written in terms of.
recorder(char_w: int = 8, line_h: int = 16) -> Recorder =
    Recorder(buf(), hits(), char_w, line_h, -1, -1, false, dark(), buf(), buf(), grabbing(),
             buf(), buf(), phases(), keyboard())

// The override in force, if anything has pushed one.
//
// **`None` is not "no colour" -- it is "the theme's"**, which is the whole of the fix: a backend
// with a default text colour of its own has one the theme cannot reach.
private override_ink(tints: Buf[u32]) -> Option[u32]
    val v = tints.view()

    if v.len == 0 then None else Some(v[v.len - 1])
end override_ink

// The rectangle two of them have in common, which is what nesting one clip inside another leaves.
//
// An empty result -- a width or a height of nothing -- is what "these do not touch" looks like, and
// `overlaps` answers false against it, which is the whole reason this does not need a special case.
private meet(a: Rect, b: Rect) -> Rect
    val x = if a.x > b.x then a.x else b.x
    val y = if a.y > b.y then a.y else b.y
    val right = if a.right() < b.right() then a.right() else b.right()
    val bottom = if a.bottom() < b.bottom() then a.bottom() else b.bottom()

    Rect(x, y, if right > x then right - x else 0, if bottom > y then bottom - y else 0)
end meet

// The smallest thing that satisfies `Canvas`: a body for every member the trait leaves without one,
// and no opinion about anything else.
//
// **It is here to say what a backend actually has to write.** Eleven one-line bodies is the whole
// obligation, and a person about to bind this toolkit to a display they own can read that in one
// screen rather than deriving it from a trait with prose between every member. `Recorder` and
// `PlutoCanvas` are both far larger than the minimum, so neither answers the question.
//
// It is also the only thing in the package that runs the trait's own default bodies. Both other
// backends override both defaults, so without this one nothing would ever check that a member with
// a body is *inherited* rather than merely declared -- and a backend somebody else writes is
// precisely this: what it had to implement, and silence about time and about pointers.
//
// **A tree painted through it is walked, measured and laid out, and draws nothing.** That is worth
// having on its own: it is the cost of the tree with the cost of the drawing taken out, which is the
// difference a benchmark is trying to see.
struct Blind end Blind

impl Canvas for Blind
    fill(*self, r: Rect, color: u32, radius: int) = ()

    stroke(*self, r: Rect, color: u32, width: int, radius: int, dash: int) = ()

    text(*self, r: Rect, t: string) = ()

    clip(*self, r: Rect) = ()

    unclip(*self) = ()

    ink(*self, color: u32) = ()

    unink(*self) = ()

    hovered(*self, r: Rect) -> bool = false

    pressed(*self, r: Rect) -> bool = false

    // **Zero rather than a guess.** A canvas that cannot measure has no business inventing a font,
    // and a layout laid out against nothing is visibly nothing rather than subtly wrong.
    text_size(*self, s: string) -> Size = Size(0, 0)

    hit(*self, r: Rect, f: &Fn() -> unit) = ()

    // A backend that draws nothing raises nothing, which is the one case where dropping an overlay
    // is the right answer rather than a missing dialog.
    raise_above(*self, v: &View, at: Rect) = ()

    take_overlays(*self) -> Buf[Overlay] = buf()
end Blind

blind() -> Blind = Blind()
